---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="skills · keel" description="The six skills keel ships, what each one does, and when it runs.">
  <header class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">skills</span>
      <h2>Six skills, one direction of trust</h2>
      <p class="sub">You ask each one a question in plain language, and each one says
      where its claims came from. Their instructions tell them not to act unless you
      ask. Two of them cover time. deck tracks what you promised and week tracks what
      happened.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid cols2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>deck: what you promised</h3>
          <p class="big">Say a commitment out loud today. Tomorrow, in a fresh session,
          ask "where do I stand?"</p>
          <p>deck keeps your promises in your own words. "Review it deeply enough that
          it can merge by Wednesday" says more than a due date field can. When you ask,
          it checks the live facts. Did that get merged? Is the ticket overdue? It flags
          promises nothing has moved on, and you can reorder the list just by saying
          so. It never writes a status down. It checks each one fresh, so none of them
          go stale.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>week: what happened</h3>
          <p class="big">Rebuild what you actually did from three sources, because git
          alone misses your most valuable work.</p>
          <p>Weighing options, reviewing someone else's change, looking into something
          and deciding against it. None of that shows up in the code you shipped. week
          combines the work diary, your project histories, and your team's review tools
          where you've connected them. Then it drafts a summary you can send. It doesn't
          make up activity, and it says "no evidence" where it finds nothing, not "no
          work".</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>guide: how it all works</h3>
          <p class="big">guide explains keel from the files installed on your machine,
          not from memory.</p>
          <p>It reads the other skills' own files, runs the CLI's help, and answers from
          what's on disk. When keel gets a new skill, guide covers it without anyone
          updating guide. In walkthrough mode it gives you a dry run of week or deck. It
          runs only the steps that read and stops before anything writes. Every
          explanation tells you how to turn the thing off.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>setup: how it's wired</h3>
          <p class="big">The install steps, as a conversation.</p>
          <p>setup looks around first and asks only about what's missing. It tells you
          about each write before it makes it. It uses the CLI's flags, so you never
          sit at an interactive prompt. It builds your project list from the work diary
          and your git remotes, and asks you only for what it can't look up. It doesn't
          delete anything, and you can run it again any time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>doctor: find it and fix it</h3>
          <p class="big">Check keel, and fix what's broken.</p>
          <p>doctor runs <code>keel doctor</code> and explains each finding in plain
          terms. With your OK, it applies the fix the check prints, then checks again
          until everything passes. The most useful check is the one for Basic Memory. It
          confirms your notes store really works, which is more than checking that a
          folder exists.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>fleet: how a repo accepts code</h3>
          <p class="big">Put a new or existing repo on your usual CI and merge
          rules.</p>
          <p>fleet reads which workflows repo and template you use from your notes.
          Then it checks whether the repo can auto-merge at all. A private repo on
          GitHub's free plan can't, and fleet's instructions tell it to say so. It tells
          you about each change before making it. Its instructions also tell it never to
          handle the review bot's token. It gives you the command to set the token
          yourself.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>the toolbox underneath</h3>
          <p class="big"><code>keel status · log · recall · doctor · setup · settings · update ·
          migrate · link</code></p>
          <p>The skills run on these small commands. Most of them only read, the ones
          that write are careful, and running any of them twice is safe. Usually the
          assistant runs them for you. <code>keel doctor</code> is the loud one. It
          checks everything the rest of keel keeps quiet about and tells you exactly
          what to fix. <a href="/technical#cli">The technical version →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
